[THOUGHT]  
The patient presents with a progressive, insidious onset of behavioral disinhibition (inappropriate laughing, foul language, impulsive grabbing), worsening over several months, now accompanied by functional decline and emerging memory impairment affecting self-care. Notably absent are classic “red flag” symptoms for other neurodegenerative or metabolic disorders — no motor signs (tremor, rigidity, gait disturbance), no seizures, no visual changes, no systemic illness markers, and no reversible causes identified on initial screening (normal TSH, no known B12/folate deficiency, negative RPR/HIV). Neuropsychological testing reveals *disproportionate deficits in executive function and language*, with relative sparing of memory and visuospatial skills — a pattern highly suggestive of **frontotemporal lobe dysfunction**, particularly the behavioral variant of frontotemporal dementia (bvFTD). The absence of family history of early-onset dementia does not rule out sporadic bvFTD (which accounts for ~60–70% of cases), though genetic testing (C9orf72, MAPT, GRN) and serum progranulin remain clinically indicated to assess for underlying pathology. MRI, FDG-PET, CSF biomarkers, and EEG are pending but will help differentiate bvFTD from Alzheimer’s disease, primary progressive aphasia, or atypical parkinsonian syndromes.

[SUMMARY]  
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Highly suspicious for behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia (bvFTD), sporadic form**, based on:  
  • Insidious onset and progressive course (>3 months) of core behavioral symptoms (disinhibition, socially inappropriate behavior, impulsivity);  
  • Early executive dysfunction and language deficits on neuropsychological testing, with relative preservation of memory and visuospatial skills;  
  • Absence of alternative explanations (no metabolic, infectious, toxic, psychiatric, or structural causes identified to date);  
  • No supportive evidence for Alzheimer’s disease (e.g., prominent amnestic syndrome), Lewy body dementia (no fluctuations, RBD, or parkinsonism), or vascular etiology (no stepwise decline or stroke risk factors).  

**Key Diagnostic Next Steps Pending:**  
- Brain MRI with contrast: To assess for frontal and/or temporal atrophy (especially orbitofrontal, dorsolateral prefrontal, anterior cingulate, and anterior temporal regions);  
- FDG-PET: Expected to show hypometabolism in frontal and anterior temporal lobes;  
- CSF biomarkers (Aβ42, t-tau, p-tau, α-synuclein): To help exclude Alzheimer’s pathology (low Aβ42, high p-tau) or synucleinopathy;  
- Serum progranulin level + genetic testing (C9orf72, MAPT, GRN): Critical for identifying pathogenic mutations or haploinsufficiency (e.g., GRN-related FTD), which may inform prognosis and family counseling;  
- EEG: To rule out non-convulsive seizures or encephalopathic patterns (though typically normal or show non-specific frontal slowing in bvFTD).  

**Recommendations:**  
- Formal referral to behavioral neurology or cognitive disorders clinic for integrated interpretation of multimodal data;  
- Caregiver education and safety assessment (e.g., driving, finances, supervision needs);  
- Consider speech-language and occupational therapy evaluation for functional support;  
- Avoid antipsychotics unless severe agitation poses safety risk (high sensitivity to adverse effects in FTD); SSRIs may be trialed cautiously for disinhibition/compulsivity if evidence supports benefit.  

No acute intervention required; diagnosis remains clinical and biomarker-supported per current international consensus criteria (Rascovsky et al., 2011).

